<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indépendance Cha Cha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">written 20 January <a href="/years/1960">1960</a>; first played 1 February <a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In 1959 the Belgian government called a Belgo-Congolese Round Table Conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> to discuss the political future of the Belgian Congo. Thomas and Philippe Kanza, sons of the Congolese delegate Daniel Kanza, proposed bringing Congolese musicians to entertain the delegates, and late in 1959 Thomas invited Joseph Kabasele, known on stage as Le Grand Kallé and leader of African Jazz, and Victor Longomba, leader of the rival band OK Jazz.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Franco Luambo of OK Jazz declined the invitation because his band had other engagements, so Victor Longomba and the OK Jazz guitarist Armando Brazzos joined Joseph Kabasele and the African Jazz members Nico Kasanda, Déchaud Mwamba and Roger Izeidi, with the conga drummer Pierre Yatula. They played under the name African Jazz, and Kabasele also took part in the discussions of the Round Table Conference.</p><p>The song drew on the instruments and rhythms of the music of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-diaspora">African diaspora</a>, particularly that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, in the Congolese rumba style. Longomba sang it and Kasanda played the guitar at its first performance at the Hotel Plaza in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. During the conference the group recorded it with Kabasele's Table Ronde and Longomba's Vive Patrice Lumumba in a studio affiliated with His Master's Voice; when that label showed no interest, Kabasele took the masters to other companies until Fonior agreed to release them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Sung in Lingala with French loanwords, the lyrics called for unity among the factions and leaders of the nationalist movement in the coming independent Congo, and the refrain credited independence wholly to the Congolese people. The verses name the acronyms of the main parties, among them ABAKO, CONAKAT, the Mouvement National Congolais, the Parti National du Progrès and the Parti Solidaire Africain, and the surnames of politicians including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-kasavubu">Joseph Kasa-Vubu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba">Patrice Lumumba</a>, Albert Kalonji, Moise Tshombe and Daniel Kanza.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The song was popular from its first broadcasts and has been called the first Pan-African hit. Radio Congo Belge carried it across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, its language made it a hit in the neighbouring French Congo, and its mood suited the many Francophone countries that became independent in 1960 or soon after. When Rwanda became independent on 1 July 1962, a youth group marched through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kigali">Kigali</a> singing it. The Belgian historian David Van Reybrouck wrote that, whatever the official anthems, it has been the one true Congolese national anthem since independence.</p><p>It is performed in Raoul Peck's 2000 film Lumumba and appears in his documentary Lumumba, la mort d'un prophète. The rapper Baloji reworked it as Le Jour d'Après on the 2011 album Kinshasa Succursale, and the Haitian band Tabou Combo released Rumba Liberté in 2011.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Indépendance Cha Cha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kabasele wrote &quot;<b>Indépendance Cha Cha</b>&quot; on 20 January 1960, in the Congolese rumba style, drawing on instruments and rhythms of the music of the African diaspora, particularly Cuba.</blockquote><a href="/songs/independance-cha-cha">Indépendance Cha Cha</a></li></ul></section>
